edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478362 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478362 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478362 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478362 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f2390c11-a776-5a6c-9a83-24a35d5a12f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478381 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478381 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478381 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}x, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22625d61-74d2-5702-9e7b-d31919b31ad0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478381 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1.5,Y\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{1}{4}x(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1.5}\\frac{7}{12}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{24}]}_{1}^{1.5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7(1.5)^{2}}{24}-\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.365.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478387 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478387 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478387 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478387 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}({y}^{2}+1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "321896e4-7ca4-574a-a1b1-f3375d212e65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478387 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143356" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478394 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "418cd51b-54c6-5702-b58c-50620816a1ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478394 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [2,4]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}, & \\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3,Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{x+1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x)]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8+4)-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{9}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478396 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478396 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478396 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478396 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}y, & \\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478396 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "427c86d9-8f85-5921-9341-2ec04eff93eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478396 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,4]\\times [2,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20}xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,2\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,1\\le Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{20}xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{20}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478414 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478414 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478414 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478414 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ef50eeb-da62-5943-922a-49e7f6ad7489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478414 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478421 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b80fc5d-3cc1-506f-9e31-9f2ad06b5cc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478421 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot [y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ and $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{4}{\\pi })}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478423 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478423 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478423 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478423 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}, & \\,0\\le y, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478423 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8523bc2d-4a4c-583b-88c5-0e1cec2367ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478423 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x-y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,0\\le y \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X,Y\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{b}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478424 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478424 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478424 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478424 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}(x+2), & \\,1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478424 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85b259be-6bf2-5d1d-9133-ee3e5619e62d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478424 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [1,3]\\times [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X\\le 2,Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2){e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{24}(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(2+4)-\\frac{1}{24}(\\frac{1}{2}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{5}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478431 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(y+1), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9efe9c4-330f-5e24-830e-56ad2ba97826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478431 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2})\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}(y+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478439 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478439 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478439 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478439 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478439 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7f5fbd8-1657-5933-82eb-1254d5fe2e87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478439 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{9}{8}(xy)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{x}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478444 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt{2x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-1), & \\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdd3bac4-4f21-5e93-9c5a-1e5e329b0b34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478444 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478458 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y{e}^{-3x},\\, & 0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eac65ba4-c3fd-5292-817a-bbb6eafdc1fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478458 #edb/ref 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-3x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2y{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{b}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{b} \\\\  & =2y{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3b}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{2y{e}^{-3x}}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
